declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-101';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 15, $j$4.3 Visions of Glory: Ezekiel$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Ezekiel 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ezekiel/1"}, {"title": "Ezekiel 34 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ezekiel/34"}, {"title": "Ezekiel 36 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ezekiel/36"}, {"title": "Ezekiel 37 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ezekiel/37"}], "body": "Read Ezekiel 1:1 to 3, Ezekiel 34, Ezekiel 36:24 to 28 and Ezekiel 37 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nEzekiel was a priest who was carried to Babylon in the first deportation, before Jerusalem fell. God called him by the river Chebar and gave him amazing visions of His glory. His book has two main parts. In the first part he warns that Jerusalem will fall because of idolatry. After the city fell, his message turned to hope. God promised to gather His people, give them a new heart and a new spirit, and put His Spirit within them. The vision of the valley of dry bones in chapter 37 is a picture of God bringing Israel back to life.\n\nKey words: vision, glory, watchman, new heart, restoration.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell where Ezekiel was when God called him. (2) Describe the valley of dry bones. (3) Explain what a new heart means in Ezekiel 36:26.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Ezekiel was a priest, but he never served in the temple. When he was a young man, Babylon carried him away to a foreign land. There, by the river Chebar, the heavens opened and Ezekiel saw visions of God. These visions show the glory and majesty of the LORD, who is not limited to a building or a city. God was with His people even in exile.\n\nGod made Ezekiel a watchman. A watchman stands on the wall and warns the people when danger is coming. Ezekiel 33 says that if the watchman sees the sword coming and blows the trumpet, the people are responsible to listen. Ezekiel's first warnings said that Jerusalem would fall, because the people had filled the temple with idols. The people in exile did not want to believe it, but the news soon came that the city had been destroyed.\n\nAfter the fall of Jerusalem, Ezekiel's message turned to hope. In chapter 34 God promised to be the Shepherd who would search for His scattered sheep. In chapter 36 God made one of the great promises of the Old Testament. Ezekiel 36:26 says, A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh. And verse 27 says, And I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes. God was promising not only to bring the people home but to change them from the inside.\n\nThen comes the most famous vision. In Ezekiel 37 the prophet was set down in a valley full of dry bones. God asked, Son of man, can these bones live? Ezekiel answered, O Lord GOD, thou knowest. At God's command Ezekiel prophesied, and the bones came together, flesh grew on them, and breath entered them. They stood up as an exceedingly great army. God explained that the bones were the whole house of Israel, and that He would bring them out of their graves and bring them home. Even a nation that felt dead could live again.\n\nThe book ends with a vision of a new temple and the words, The LORD is there. The God who seemed to be far away would one day dwell with His people again.\n\nYour task is to tell where Ezekiel was when God called him, describe the valley of dry bones, and explain what a new heart means. Then take the four question check.\n\nGod can bring life where everything seems dead. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.3 Visions of Glory: Ezekiel$j$);
end
$seed$;
